{
  "query": "ALS vs post-polio syndrome differential diagnosis EMG interpretation",
  "results": [
    {
      "title": "Post-polio syndrome vs progressive motor neuron disease: key distinctions",
      "finding": "Post-polio syndrome: LMN only, no UMN signs (Babinski/Hoffmann not present), restricted to originally affected body regions, very slow progression. Progressive motor neuron disease: combined UMN+LMN, spreads beyond the original distribution, progressive. New denervation in body regions not affected by polio is the EMG key to identifying a superimposed motor neuron disease."
    },
    {
      "title": "Incidence of progressive motor neuron disease in post-polio patients",
      "finding": "The incidence of motor neuron disease may be modestly increased in polio survivors (possibly related to baseline motor neuron vulnerability). When clinical features suggest progressive motor neuron disease beyond post-polio syndrome, EMG in non-polio-affected regions is diagnostically informative."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Post-polio syndrome is characterized by new or worsening weakness, fatigue, and muscle atrophy in muscle groups previously affected by polio, with very slow progression; it is a lower motor neuron condition. A progressive motor neuron disease is distinguished by combined upper and lower motor neuron involvement and spread to body regions not originally affected by polio. EMG examination of body regions unaffected by original polio can help identify new denervation beyond the expected post-polio distribution. The incidence of motor neuron disease may be modestly increased in polio survivors."
}

