      "title": "C9orf72 motor neuron disease with frontal-network neurodegeneration: clinical spectrum and management",
      "finding": "C9orf72 expansion is the most common cause of both familial motor neuron disease (~40%) and familial frontal-network neurodegeneration (~25%). Approximately 15% of motor neuron disease patients have behavioral/cognitive features of frontal-network neurodegeneration. The combined syndrome is a unified TDP-43 proteinopathy. Prognosis is worse than either condition in isolation."
    },
    {
      "title": "Management of motor neuron disease with concurrent frontal-network neurodegeneration",
      "finding": "Disease-modifying and symptomatic pharmacotherapy used in isolated motor neuron disease is continued when frontal-network neurodegeneration co-occurs. Behavioral symptoms of frontal-network neurodegeneration are typically managed with SSRIs and low-dose antipsychotics. Published guidelines recommend early assessment of decision-making capacity in patients with concurrent cognitive impairment."
    }
  ],
  "summary": ""
}

