  ],
  "summary": "Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome (WKS) can cause behavioral changes and dementia in patients with chronic alcohol use. Key distinguishing features: (1) MRI shows mammillary body atrophy and thalamic T2 signal change (periaqueductal gray involvement in acute Wernicke's phase); (2) thiamine level is low or borderline; (3) behavioral profile is predominantly amnestic (Korsakoff amnesia) rather than the disinhibition and social dysfunction characteristic of bvFTD; (4) MoCA typically shows severe memory impairment with relative frontal preservation, the opposite of bvFTD profile. Clinical correlation with neuroimaging and thiamine level is required to distinguish these entities."
}

