      "title": "Cranial nerve involvement preceding limb weakness in acute inflammatory polyneuropathy",
      "summary": "Unilateral lower motor neuron facial palsy has been reported as an early cranial nerve manifestation of acute inflammatory polyradiculoneuropathy in case series, preceding ascending limb weakness by days to two weeks. Subsequent development of global areflexia and limb weakness characterizes an evolving demyelinating polyradiculoneuropathy.",
      "relevance": "HIGH"
    },
    {
      "title": "Cervical cord T2 signal in acute inflammatory polyneuropathy: Wallerian degeneration versus concomitant myelopathy",
      "summary": "Ill-defined T2 cord signal at the cervicomedullary junction has been documented in severe GBS. Proposed mechanism involves Wallerian degeneration of anterior horn cell axons under severe demyelinating stress. Features reported in association include: absence of a discrete enhancing cord plaque on MRI, albumino-cytologic dissociation without pleocytosis, peripheral demyelinating pattern on NCS, and negative aquaporin-4 antibody.",
      "relevance": "HIGH"
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Facial palsy preceding limb weakness is a recognized early cranial nerve manifestation in a subset of GBS cases. Cervicomedullary junction T2 signal has been reported in severe GBS and is distinguishable from primary transverse myelitis by the absence of a discrete enhancing cord plaque and the presence of peripheral electrodiagnostic features."
}

