    },
    {
      "title": "McDonald 2017 criteria: practical application and common pitfalls",
      "source": "Multiple Sclerosis Journal 2022",
      "key_finding": "A single periventricular lesion with Dawson finger morphology should raise suspicion for MS even if other criteria are not initially met. Follow-up imaging at 3-6 months is recommended when initial findings are inconclusive."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Migraine-related white matter lesions are common but typically differ from MS lesions in size, morphology, and distribution. Periventricular lesions perpendicular to the ventricle (Dawson fingers) are more specific for MS. OCB-negative MS occurs in 5-10% of cases. When initial findings are equivocal, serial MRI demonstrating dissemination in time is a key diagnostic strategy per McDonald 2017 criteria."
