      "authors": "Shin HW, Chung SJ",
      "journal": "Journal of Clinical Neurology",
      "year": "2012",
      "key_finding": "DIP is typically symmetric, develops within days to weeks of drug exposure, lacks non-motor prodromal features, and resolves within weeks to months after offending agent discontinuation"
    },
    {
      "title": "DaTscan Utility in Differentiating Neurodegenerative from Non-Neurodegenerative Parkinsonism",
      "authors": "Bajaj N, Hauser RA, Grachev ID",
      "journal": "Movement Disorders",
      "year": "2013",
      "key_finding": "DaTscan (Ioflupane I-123 SPECT) reliably distinguishes neurodegenerative parkinsonism (abnormal scan) from drug-induced parkinsonism (normal scan) with sensitivity 78-97% and specificity 80-100%"
    },
    {
      "title": "Prodromal Markers of Parkinson Disease",
      "authors": "Berg D, et al.",
      "journal": "Nature Reviews Neurology",
      "year": "2021",
      "key_finding": "REM sleep behavior disorder, hyposmia, constipation, and depression may precede motor symptoms by years. Presence of multiple prodromal markers strongly supports underlying synucleinopathy rather than drug-induced parkinsonism"
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The literature supports a systematic approach to differentiating PD from DIP: (1) assess symmetry of findings \u2014 PD is typically asymmetric, DIP symmetric; (2) evaluate prodromal non-motor features \u2014 presence of RBD, hyposmia, and constipation favors PD; (3) DaTscan is the most useful ancillary test, with abnormal results confirming presynaptic dopaminergic deficit and effectively ruling out pure DIP; (4) discontinuation of the offending agent with clinical monitoring over 6 months is recommended regardless."
}

