{
  "query": "SCN9A Nav1.7 gain-of-function hereditary small fiber neuropathy treatment sodium channel",
  "results": [
    {
      "citation": "Faber CG et al. (2012) J Neurol.",
      "summary": "Nav1.7 p.Ile739Val causes constitutive channel activation, DRG neuron hyperexcitability, episodic pain in 3 family members. Temperature sensitivity explains fever-triggered episodes."
    },
    {
      "citation": "Faber CG et al. (2015) Brain 138:1888-1901.",
      "summary": "Lacosamide (slow sodium channel inactivator) reduces Nav1.7 gain-of-function and provides superior pain relief vs standard treatments in SCN9A-positive patients. Carbamazepine and mexiletine used with variable success. Standard gabapentin/pregabalin less effective for channelopathy-SFN."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "SCN9A gain-of-function variants (including p.Ile739Val) are associated with hereditary small fiber neuropathy and dysautonomia with autosomal dominant inheritance. Temperature-sensitive channel kinetics underlie fever-triggered pain episodes observed in some family members. Evidence for sodium channel-directed pharmacotherapy (lacosamide, carbamazepine, mexiletine) in SCN9A-positive small fiber neuropathy is derived from case series and small open-label trials. Fabry disease (GLA mutations) is the most common treatable hereditary cause of small fiber neuropathy and should be excluded before attributing disease to sodium channelopathy."
}

