{
  "query": "Cocaine use aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage mechanism management",
  "results": [
    {
      "finding": "Cocaine increases SAH risk 5-28 fold through catecholamine surge and acute hypertension"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Pre-existing aneurysm is typical - cocaine is the precipitant not the cause of aneurysm formation"
    },
    {
      "finding": "BP management in cocaine SAH: avoid labetalol (minimal alpha blockade) - use phentolamine or nicardipine"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Avoid pure beta-blockers in cocaine toxidrome - paradoxical hypertension from unopposed alpha stimulation"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Addiction counseling and cessation essential after recovery"
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Cocaine is associated with a substantially elevated risk of subarachnoid hemorrhage, attributed to catecholamine-mediated acute hypertensive surges that may precipitate rupture of a pre-existing intracranial aneurysm. Pharmacological management of acute cocaine-related hypertension favors agents with combined alpha- and beta-adrenergic blockade or calcium channel blockade; pure beta-adrenergic blockade may result in unopposed alpha stimulation and paradoxical worsening of hypertension. Standard evidence-based management of aneurysmal SAH applies once the acute hypertensive crisis is treated."
}

