      "finding": "Hyponatremia reduces renal lithium clearance - increases lithium levels toward toxic range"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Lithium toxicity symptoms: coarse tremor, nystagmus, ataxia, confusion, seizures"
    },
    {
      "finding": "Monitor lithium levels every 24h in SAH patients on lithium, especially if sodium changes"
    },
    {
      "finding": "If lithium toxic, hydration and sodium replacement will help clear; dialysis for severe toxicity"
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage frequently causes hyponatremia through cerebral salt wasting or SIADH. Hyponatremia reduces renal lithium clearance, raising serum lithium concentrations toward the toxic range. Published case series and pharmacokinetic data support monitoring lithium levels at least every 24\u201348 hours in patients on lithium who develop SAH, with more frequent sampling if sodium falls. Lithium toxicity (coarse tremor, nystagmus, ataxia, confusion) may be clinically difficult to distinguish from delayed cerebral ischemia."
}

