  "results": [
    {
      "finding": "Levetiracetam is commonly used as first-line antiseizure medication in GBM-related epilepsy; lacosamide and valproate have also been evaluated in this population."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Non-convulsive status epilepticus in patients with glioblastoma is underdiagnosed; EEG is required for detection, as clinical examination alone is insufficient."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Elevated rCBV on perfusion MRI has been reported to discriminate tumor recurrence from radiation necrosis with approximately 87% sensitivity."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Corticosteroid dose reduction is associated with increased peritumoral edema and may correlate with increased seizure frequency in some glioblastoma series."
    },
    {
      "finding": "Bevacizumab, a VEGF inhibitor, has been associated with reduced seizure frequency in recurrent GBM, potentially through reduction of vascular permeability and peritumoral edema."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Tumor-related epilepsy occurs in 30\u201350% of patients with glioblastoma; non-convulsive seizure activity is frequently underdiagnosed in this population and EEG monitoring is required for detection. Peritumoral edema from recurrent disease is a recognized precipitant of increased seizure frequency. High rCBV on perfusion MRI has been reported to distinguish tumor recurrence from radiation necrosis with sensitivity of approximately 87%. Antiseizure medications including levetiracetam and lacosamide are used in GBM-related epilepsy; valproate has also been studied in this context."
}

